    "learningObjective": "Master the special procedure governing civil litigation involving the Government and public officers under Sections 79 to 82 and Order XXVII, focusing on the mandatory two-month statutory notice under Section 80 and the urgent relief proviso.",
    "introduction": "Suits against the State require special procedural safeguards to allow the administration an opportunity to examine claims and settle disputes without unnecessary litigation. Sections 79 to 82 and Order XXVII prescribe the statutory framework.",
    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. Formal Representation in Suits [Section 79]</h3>\n<p>In a suit by or against the Government, the authority to be named as plaintiff or defendant shall be:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In the case of the <strong>Central Government</strong>: The <strong>Union of India</strong>.</li>\n  <li>In the case of a <strong>State Government</strong>: The <strong>State</strong> (e.g., State of Uttar Pradesh).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Mandatory Statutory Notice of Two Months [Section 80(1)]</h3>\n<p>No suit shall be instituted against the Government (Central or State) or against a public officer in respect of any act purporting to be done by such public officer in his official capacity, until the expiration of <strong>two months next after notice in writing</strong> has been delivered to or left at the office of:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>In the case of Central Government (other than railways): A <strong>Secretary to that Government</strong>.</li>\n  <li>In the case of Railways: The <strong>General Manager</strong> of that railway.</li>\n  <li>In the case of State Government: A <strong>Secretary to that Government or the Collector</strong> of the district.</li>\n  <li>In the case of a Public Officer: Delivered to him or left at his office.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Object:</em> To give the Government an opportunity to scrutinize the claim, seek legal advice, and if thought fit, settle the dispute without dragging the State into costly litigation (<em>Bihari Chowdhary</em>).</p>\n\n<h3>3. Urgent or Immediate Relief Exception [Section 80(2)]</h3>\n<p>Inserted by 1976 Amendment; a suit to obtain an <strong>urgent or immediate relief</strong> against the Government or any public officer may be instituted, with the <strong>leave of the Court</strong>, without serving any notice under sub-section (1).</p>\n<p><strong>Crucial Restriction (Proviso to S. 80(2)):</strong> The Court shall <strong>NOT grant relief in the suit, whether interim or otherwise, except after giving the Government a reasonable opportunity of showing cause</strong>! If after hearing the court is satisfied that no urgent relief is needed, it shall return the plaint for presentation after complying with Section 80(1).</p>\n\n<h3>4. Execution of Decrees Against Government [Section 82]</h3>\n<p>Where a decree is passed against the Union of India or a State Government, execution shall NOT be issued on such decree unless it <strong>remains unsatisfied for a period of three months</strong> from the date of the decree!</p>\n",
